<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.6.6: Interpreting Negative Exponents and Scientific Notation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01338</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.6.6: Interpreting Negative Exponents and Scientific Notation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>df460cdb-d44b-4989-badd-19d76254b222</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>The equation \(p=5,000\cdot2^t\) represents the population of an invasive fish species in a large lake, \(t\) years since 2005, when the fish population in the lake was first surveyed.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> What was the population in 2005? 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 5,000, because \(5,000\cdot 2^0=5,000\).
  </item>
  
  <item> What is this value of the population in 2005 in scientific notation? 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(5.0\times 10^3\)
  </item>
  
  <item> For this model, what does it mean when \(t\) is –2? 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> It means 2 years before 2005, which is 2003.
  </item>
  
  <item> For \( t=-2\), is the fish population more or less than 1,000? How do you know? 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> More than 1,000. For \(t=-2\) yeilds \(5,000\cdot 2^{-2}=1,250\).
  </item>
  
  <item> Write the value of the fish population when \(t=-2\) in scientific notation. 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(1.25\times 10^3
    \)
  </item>
</list>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>Points to emphasize</title>
<para>If students struggle to interpret negative exponents, use student work from this cool-down to guide a brief discussion on the topic in a subsequent lesson.</para></section>
</content>
